WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.040
You know- whether it's a game or not is

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.770
another funny thing but I call it a no

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.620
player game. I read a book called

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.349
Automata Studies, a whole series of

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:12.480
interesting things about automatic

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.710
machines, the automaton. In particular,

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.670
John von Neumann had been interested in

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.619
colonising the planets. You see, you can't

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:26.449
afford to take humans out to the planets;

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:28.980
it's a long way away, you've got to carry

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.650
more food and everything than the weight

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.630
of the human by a long way. By the way,

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.989
you've also got to equip your planet

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:39.030
with an atmosphere and von Neumann's

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:43.260
idea was that you first send some

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.969
machines over. His model was that you're

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:51.120
really going to send them to Mars; and

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.739
Mars is called the Red Planet,

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:55.680
and that's because it's got a lot of

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.859
iron ore, basically rust. And so what

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:02.370
these machines do is initially, well a

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.710
whole lot of stages but a big

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.220
important stage, is they smelt the iron

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.310
ore and get iron. And then, they're quite

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.950
clever machines, they start using this

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.799
iron ore to build new machines whose job

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:19.860
is to smelt more iron ore and so on.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.490
Not only are you doing that but rust

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.509
is iron oxide and so when you separate

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:30.810
this into the iron you get 
oxygen out of it,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.270
and so now you can build up an

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.520
atmosphere containing oxygen. I don't

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.430
think he helped to equip the whole of

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.130
Mars with such an atmosphere, but maybe

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.460
you have some kind of shell or dome and

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.899
in that dome you can have an atmosphere.

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:49.079
And then after considerable time when

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:51.329
these machines have all been
chugging away and

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:53.689
making more machines and so on and

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:56.159
smelting more iron and producing more

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:59.640
oxygen you send the guys over -us, you

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.960
know. Then he realised that you had to

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.740
have a machine capable of building a

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.890
copy of itself, and that seems a bit

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.740
difficult.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.050
Maybe in order to build a machine you

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.510
need a more complicated machine; and in

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.250
order to build that more complicated

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.170
machine you still need an even more

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.160
complicated machine. But von Neumann

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.270
thought no maybe that's not so, and and

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.180
he proved it wasn't so. He proved it,

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.190
really, I don't know whether he was aware

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.030
of this by copying what's done to us

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:42.260
with RNA and DNA. Inside every cell of

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:46.520
your body you've got some RNA molecules

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.670
and they contain complete instructions

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:52.700
for building another one, a copy of you. I

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.840
call that the tape; you know, so

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.790
you have a machine and you put a tape in

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.860
this and it builds a copy of whatever

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.870
machine is specified by that tape. And

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.670
then if you can do that, and it's fairly

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.220
easy to do that in a way, you can now

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.750
feed it with a copy of its own tape and

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.490
then you can build- in that way you can

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.150
manage to design a machine and a tape

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.070
which will build a copy of that machine

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:25.070
and copy its own tapes as it will build a

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:28.010
copy of itself. Von Neumann's
machine, each

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:32.900
square had 29 states - in the game of life

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.630
you only have two states, on and off, or

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.400
live and dead whatever you want to call

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.440
them. In von Neumann's you had

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.110
29 states, if you wanted it to do

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.720
something different, to have some other

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.330
facility, just added a few more states. In

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:50.330
other words his machine was designed. Now

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.450
my life game wasn't designed, I just sort

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.270
of thought if you couldn't predict what

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:59.180
it did then probably that's because it

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.590
was capable of doing anything.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.550
For about 18 months of coffee times - and

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:08.360
I'm not sure that you know we used- I'm

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.220
sure that we didn't use every coffee

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:15.770
time - we tinkered with the rules. The

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.100
rules as they finally came out where: if

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:22.430
you have exactly three live- if you're

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.850
empty or dead or whatever it is

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.530
and you have exactly three live

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.810
neighbours then something gets born

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:33.690
there at the next time. If you are alive

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.250
and you have two or three live

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.500
neighbours then you survive; I

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.600
mentioned a birth rule first, to be

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.960
born you need exactly three live

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.480
neighbours, to survive you need either

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.790
two or three. 
- (Brady: So there were other)

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.260
(iterations of the game, it could have)

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.690
(been different?)
- Yeah indeed; you know, what

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:55.150
was different for quite a long time we

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.700
tinkered with these rules and finally

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.310
came up with the ones I said. And they

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:03.280
really seemed to have very nice

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.250
properties, namely didn't seem to be able

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.160
to predict what would happen. And

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.920
in the end we succeeded in proving

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.330
essentially anything could happen, these

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.100
things could do any kind of computation you

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.510
wanted to do. You could design

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.940
configurations that rebuilt themselves

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:24.490
or built more complicated machines than

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.950
themselves, all sorts of things. 
- (You had)

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:28.300
(no computers when you were doing this)

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.450
(early work?)
- No computers at all. After a

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:34.330
time there was a computer which had a

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.930
screen, the PDP8, and before it the

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.560
PDP7; I've forgotten what those PDPs-

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.420
probably the DP is data processing or

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.490
something. And then somebody immediately

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.870
programmed the game of life for it.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:52.360
But the initial thinking was all done

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.060
before then. I told Martin Gardner about

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.380
it, he's the person who ran this

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:58.000
mathematical games column in the

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.510
Scientific American for 25 or 30 years. I

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.820
thought it would interest people, I

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:05.230
didn't think it would interest people-

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.630
his readers as much as it actually did.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:10.180
Well first of all his first column in

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:12.070
the Scientific American was considerably

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:15.010
before this about hexaflexagons,

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:20.620
and it got more reader mail than any

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.290
previous article in the entire hundred

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:26.110
and something year old history of the

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:28.060
magazine up to that time, and so the

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.330
editors wanted him to write a monthly

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:36.220
column. And he said he said to me that he

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.880
was so broke that he just said yes

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.170
before he knew whether he could write a

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.110
monthly column. But anyway he wrote that

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.150
monthly column very successfully and

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.400
then when the life game came up

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:49.330
guess what?

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:52.120
That got more reader correspondence that

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.250
anything in the entire history of the

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.370
magazine including this hexaflexagon

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:59.650
column. From my point of view though it

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:02.160
wasn't real mathematics. It was

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.560
flattering to have so many readers

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:10.300
interested in it and so on and but I I

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.520
personally didn't think all that much of

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.100
it, but it's nice you know, it's nice to

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.340
have other people value something that

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:20.430
you know I didn't really value in a way.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:23.440
Overall now that I'm getting old I'm

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.160
really very pleased that this did happen,

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.770
it's one incident in my mathematical

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:34.120
life and I shouldn't be so annoyed about

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.810
it and I'm trying not to be.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.300
(Has it been built upon? Is it one of-)

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.760
(the mathematics and-)
- No it's

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.770
finished. I mean that's another thing

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.410
really; you can build upon it in the

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.290
following sense, you can study particular

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.460
configurations, you can find the

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.350
alternative rules that still have the

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:57.580
same properties and so on - but nothing I

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.830
think that followed on, it was just as

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:02.350
interesting as the basic fact that this

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:06.060
set of rules did exist fairly simple and

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.980
have these astonishing properties which

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:10.780
weren't astonishing to me. Some

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.390
configurations just died off, after a

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.430
time there's nothing left on the board.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.770
Some configurations seem to go on

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:21.520
forever. Can you tell which of those is

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.060
going to happen? Well not really. If you

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:29.320
if you put put a configuration on the

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.480
board and follow it and follow it and

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:34.180
follow it, and after a thousand moves it

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:35.950
hasn't died off,

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:37.870
oh well maybe it's going to die off next

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.240
move. There's no way of telling, no

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.519
algorithmic way of telling whether a

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:44.670
thing is definitely going to die off.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:47.230
Following it you see doesn't tell you. If

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.630
you follow it for a thousand moves and

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.810
it hasn't died off yet well maybe it'll

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.639
die off in a million moves or a billion

00:08:54.639 --> 00:08:57.130
moves or a gazillion moves - whatever that

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:01.810
is. And we do know - and this is not

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:05.680
due to me its a theorem of mathematical

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.370
logic dating from the 1930s - the Halting

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:12.310
Problem, there's no way of absolutely

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:14.860
guaranteeing to tell whether a thing

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:16.690
will go on forever or fade away

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:18.730
completely, that's one of the astonishing

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.100
properties. You would think that if a

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.820
thing is governed by very simple rules

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.110
there would be a very simple way of

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:29.019
telling whether this very simple thing

00:09:29.019 --> 00:09:31.420
is a consequence of those very simple

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.730
rules, but there isn't a way of telling.

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:35.470
(Has the inventor of game of life or)

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:37.870
(people who've studied a deeply have a)

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.639
(deeper understanding? Like can you look)

00:09:39.639 --> 00:09:42.930
(at a configuration and see something 
or does it still baffle you too?)

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.199
Well I might be able to 
sort of recognise some

00:09:47.199 --> 00:09:49.180
little portion of it and say, well that's

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:50.769
not going to have any effect we can

00:09:50.769 --> 00:09:52.990
proceed as if that's not there because

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:56.230
it's going to die in 23 moves anyway. But

00:09:56.230 --> 00:10:00.240
no in general no you can't. I mean this

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.010
condition, you know, you cannot tell is

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:06.490
not a question of not being able to tell

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:08.170
because you haven't got a big enough

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:11.800
brain or something; it is an absolute

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.279
condition you know doesn't matter how
clever you are,

00:10:14.279 --> 00:10:17.050
there's no guaranteed way of telling.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:20.500
Okay, I don't hate it.
- (But you don't love it?)

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:23.480
No I don't love it, I really don't.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:28.200
Yes it made me happy, it's the only one of

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:32.760
those different things. I, you know, I sit

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.130
in a corridor in the mathematics

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:39.180
department in Princeton and I think

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:43.440
about things and I imagine that the

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.690
young graduate students there
think, you know,

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.339
this guy's a loony, he did something good

00:10:47.339 --> 00:10:51.480
once and I don't care,
I really don't care.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:54.120
I've been released from worrying about

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.100
what other people think about about me.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:59.490
I'm often said, I've said for 25 or 30

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:02.670
years, that the one thing I'd really like

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:06.320
to know before I die is-

